    "q-81146": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81146",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?",
      "worked_solution": "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{\\text{i}}^{2}+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}(2-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 2(2-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-2\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(2-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(2-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
